WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.349 --> 00:00:02.932
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.975
- White corn is one of our relatives.

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:12.111
We feel that she is alive, she wants

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:15.875
to do the job that she told
our creator she would do,

00:00:15.875 --> 00:00:19.798
is to feed our people and nourish them.

00:00:19.798 --> 00:00:22.761
The work that we do, if
we didn't have this,

00:00:22.761 --> 00:00:24.844
we would lose everything.

00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:34.738
- For a few thousand years,
Potawatomi people

00:00:34.738 --> 00:00:37.475
have been incredible
farmers and have grown

00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:39.611
lots and lots of varieties of crops.

00:00:39.611 --> 00:00:41.622
The seed library is a place where

00:00:41.622 --> 00:00:44.850
we take crops which are disappearing,

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.837
rare and endangered
Potawatomi vegetables,

00:00:47.837 --> 00:00:50.151
and we grow them out, and we restore them

00:00:50.151 --> 00:00:54.234
and put them back in the
hands of native farmers.

00:01:04.574 --> 00:01:07.738
George Martin carries
the Potawatomi tradition

00:01:07.738 --> 00:01:10.488
of making ndamnabo, or corn soup.

00:01:12.336 --> 00:01:15.350
In our seed library, we have 76 unique

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.600
varieties of corn.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.410
Each one has its own cultural identity,

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.062
its own unique flavor.

00:01:21.062 --> 00:01:22.400
- After a while, when we stir it,

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.256
you'll see how tough it gets to stir it.

00:01:26.256 --> 00:01:27.856
- Our corn is a lot different than

00:01:27.856 --> 00:01:30.523
other corns which are out there.

00:01:32.356 --> 00:01:34.640
If we didn't have the seed library,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.807
these traditional food,
might be gone forever.

00:01:42.266 --> 00:01:45.750
Within our seed library,
80% of the varieties

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:50.366
which we take care of are
rare or nearly extinct.

00:01:50.366 --> 00:01:52.676
Some of the varieties we care for here,

00:01:52.676 --> 00:01:56.759
we're the only place in
the world which has them.

00:01:59.715 --> 00:02:02.422
This one's gonna be a nice one.

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:05.241
Four years ago, when we
first got nugasignuga,

00:02:05.241 --> 00:02:08.378
or White Flower Corn, we only
had a half a canning jar,

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:10.230
a very small amount of seed.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.528
We've grown it every year since, and now

00:02:12.528 --> 00:02:15.940
we have enough that we
may have 100 bushels full.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.103
That's enough that next year we can

00:02:18.103 --> 00:02:21.343
plant six or seven
acres of just this corn.

00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:23.353
That's a huge increase.

00:02:23.353 --> 00:02:26.866
- It is important, that's what
we were sent down here for,

00:02:26.866 --> 00:02:30.566
to take care of Mother Earth
and all her creations here,

00:02:30.566 --> 00:02:32.899
and that corn realizes that.

00:02:33.743 --> 00:02:36.016
We're back using it again.

00:02:36.016 --> 00:02:38.179
You know, we're gonna use it in a feast.

00:02:38.179 --> 00:02:40.130
It makes the corn feel good, after being

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.213
used by our people again.

00:02:43.393 --> 00:02:45.593
It’s done, it’s ready to eat.

00:02:45.593 --> 00:02:48.760
(inspirational music)

